'The "before and after" — a worked refactoring</h3>\n<p>Here\'s a real function that was "working" but had multiple smells. Trace the improvement:</p>\n<pre style="background:#f4f4f4; padding:1rem; overflow-x:auto; font-size:.85rem;">\n// BEFORE: long function, duplicate code, magic numbers, deep nesting\nfunction process(data, type) {\n  let result = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {\n    if (type === \'active\') {\n      if (data[i].status === 3) {\n        if (data[i].age > 18) {\n          result.push(data[i]);\n        }\n      }\n    } else if (type === \'all\') {\n      if (data[i].status === 3) {\n        result.push(data[i]);\n      }\n    } else if (type === \'minors\') {\n      if (data[i].status === 3) {\n        if (data[i].age <= 18) {\n          result.push(data[i]);\n        }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return result;\n}\n</pre>\n<p><strong>Smells identified:</strong> long function, duplicate <code>status === 3</code> check, magic number <code>3</code>, deep nesting, unclear name (<code>process</code>).</p>\n<pre style="background:#f4f4f4; padding:1rem; overflow-x:auto; font-size:.85rem;">\n// AFTER: extracted functions, named constants, guard clauses, clear names\nconst STATUS_APPROVED = 3;\n\nfunction isApproved(user) {\n  return user.status === STATUS_APPROVED;\n}\n\nfunction filterUsers(users, predicate) {\n  return users.filter(predicate);\n}\n\nfunction getActiveAdults(users) {\n  return filterUsers(users, u => isApproved(u) && u.age > 18);\n}\n\nfunction getApprovedUsers(users) {\n  return filterUsers(users, u => isApproved(u));\n}\n\nfunction getApprovedMinors(users) {\n  return filterUsers(users, u => isApproved(u) && u.age <= 18);\n}\n\nfunction filterByType(users, type) {\n  if (type === \'active\') return getActiveAdults(users);\n  if (type === \'all\') return getApprovedUsers(users);\n  if (type === \'minors\') return getApprovedMinors(users);\n  return [];\n}\n</pre>\n<p><strong>What improved:</strong> each function has one job and a clear name. The <code>status === 3</code> check exists in one place. Nesting is gone. A new developer can read <code>getActiveAdults</code> and understand it without reading the implementation. This is maintainability (<a href="#sec-the-parameters-quality-attributes-what-you-measure">§4</a>) in action.</p>\n\n'